<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> The Nicomachean Ethics is best understood as:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">A dialogue between Aristotle and his student Alexander the Great</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">A set of lecture notes, likely compiled and edited after Aristotle's death</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">A polished treatise that Aristotle wrote for publication</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">A commentary on Plato's Republic written by Aristotle late in life</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Nicomachean Ethics reads like what it is: lecture notes, probably edited by Aristotle's son Nicomachus or his students after his death. That explains its compressed, sometimes repetitive style.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about where the text came from. Its compressed style, repetitions, and abrupt transitions reflect its origin as material for teaching, not a finished book prepared for readers.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Aristotle's worldview is described as teleological. What does this mean?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">He believes morality is whatever produces the greatest pleasure</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">He believes the universe was created for humans</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">He believes all knowledge comes from religious revelation</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">He believes every living thing has a purpose or end built into its nature</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For Aristotle, every living thing has a telos, an end built into its nature. Understanding what something is means understanding what it is for, and this is the backbone of his ethics.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Teleology comes from the Greek telos, meaning end or goal. Ask what Aristotle thinks a thing's nature tells you about what it is for.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> Why does Aristotle argue that "flourishing" is a better translation of eudaimonia than "happiness"?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Because eudaimonia is a condition of a whole life, not a mood or feeling that comes and goes</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Because flourishing is easier to achieve than happiness</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Because flourishing is a feeling, while happiness is an activity</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Because happiness requires wealth, while flourishing does not</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Eudaimonia describes how a whole life goes, not a passing mood. You can feel happy on a given afternoon, but flourishing is something you achieve, or fail to, over a lifetime.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The issue is about time and stability. Ask whether eudaimonia is something that comes and goes from hour to hour, or something that characterizes a life as a whole.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> What role does the "hierarchy of ends" argument play in Book I?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">It shows that humans have no natural purpose</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It shows that wealth is the highest good because all other goods require it</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">It shows that there must be a final good we pursue for its own sake, or our actions would be pointless</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">It shows that everything humans do must aim at pleasure</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. If every good were pursued only for the sake of some further good, the chain would never end and desire would be empty. So there must be a final good we choose for its own sake, and Aristotle argues it is eudaimonia.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Follow the chain of 'for the sake of.' Aristotle's point is about what would happen to our choices if every end were only a means to something further.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> In the function argument, what does Aristotle identify as the distinctive function of human beings?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Perception and feeling</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Activity of the soul in accordance with reason</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Nutrition and growth</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">The pursuit of pleasure</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Nutrition and growth we share with plants, and perception with animals. What is distinctive of human beings is rational activity, so the human good is activity of the soul in accordance with reason, performed well.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The function argument looks for what is distinctive of human beings. Ask which of these capacities we share with plants or animals, and which belongs to us alone.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> An argument is valid when:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Its conclusion follows logically from its premises, regardless of whether the premises are actually true</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Its conclusion is something most people believe</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">It has been accepted by philosophers for a long time</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">All of its premises are true</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Validity is about structure: if the premises were true, the conclusion would have to be true. Whether the premises actually are true is a separate question, which is where soundness comes in.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Validity and truth are different properties. Revisit the Logic for Ethics page in Start Here: validity concerns the connection between premises and conclusion, not whether the premises are true.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> Why does Aristotle reject honor as the chief good?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Because honor is a feeling rather than an activity</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Because honor is morally wrong</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Because only wealthy people can achieve honor</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Because honor depends on other people's recognition, and what we really want is to be worthy of honor, which turns out to mean being virtuous</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Honor sits in the hands of those who give it, so it is too superficial to be the chief good. And people seek honor to be assured of their own goodness, which shows that virtue matters more than the recognition of it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aristotle's objection is not that honor is wrong. Ask where honor comes from, and what people who seek it are really trying to establish about themselves.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> According to Aristotle, how are moral virtues acquired?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Through habituation: repeated practice of the relevant actions over time</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">They are innate and do not need to be acquired</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Through formal instruction and reading</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Through pure contemplation of the good</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Moral virtues come from habit. We become just by doing just acts and brave by doing brave acts, until acting well becomes second nature.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Book II distinguishes how intellectual virtues and moral virtues are acquired. Remember the lyre player: some excellences can be taught, but others are only built by doing.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> What is a hexis?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">A temporary emotional state</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">A specific action performed at a specific time</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">A stable disposition to feel, think, and act in a certain way</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">A natural talent one is born with</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A hexis is a settled state of character, a stable disposition to feel and act in certain ways. Virtues are hexeis, which is why a single good act does not make someone virtuous.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A hexis is not a passing feeling or a single action. Ask what Aristotle calls the stable, habituated condition that virtue turns out to be.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> On Aristotle's account, courage is the mean between:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Weakness and strength</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Cowardice (too much fear) and rashness (too little fear)</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Cowardice and pride</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Fear and hope</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Courage concerns fear and confidence. The coward fears too much, the rash person too little, and the courageous person feels and acts as the situation actually calls for.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Each virtue is a mean between a vice of excess and a vice of deficiency in the same feeling. Ask what feeling courage governs, and what having too much or too little of it would look like.</div>
  </div>
</div>

